Theorem CH-102: refinement engine bootstrap (DP-10 removal soundness)

  Preconditions:
    - DP-10 was a former decision predicate at Annex E §AIMS
      claiming Owned and Linear and Once implies Uniqueness equal
      to Unique; this predicate was REMOVED as unsound per
      Annex E §AIMS DP-10 historical note
    - the refinement engine consumes post-condition implies
      pre-condition implications per the proof-checker design
      sec-Engine-per-Category-Inventory RL row
    - DP-10 removal soundness is the claim that the absence of
      DP-10 does NOT under-approximate uniqueness; i.e. every
      genuinely unique value still has Uniqueness equal to Unique
      via the remaining derivation paths (TF-3 fresh allocation
      and IC-3 fixpoint convergence and IC-2 parameter contract)
    - Annex E §AIMS DP-10 cure clarifies that uniqueness is
      established ONLY via the Uniqueness dimension OR fresh
      allocation; backward analysis proves no future duplication
      but NOT no existing aliases

  Soundness property:
    Forall v in vars.
      (Access(v) = Owned and Consumption(v) = Linear and
       Cardinality(v) = Once) does NOT imply Uniqueness(v) = Unique;
      i.e. the former DP-10 implication is unsound; its removal is
        sound; remaining derivation paths preserve the property
        "every value the calculus genuinely proves unique still
        has Uniqueness equal to Unique".

  Proof obligation:
    Refinement argument: post-condition (DP-10 removed) implies
    pre-condition (every genuinely unique value still has
    Uniqueness equal to Unique).

      Counterexample to former DP-10 (witness of unsoundness):
        Let v be a parameter passed by an upstream caller with
        Uniqueness = MaybeShared (caller may hold one or more
        upstream aliases that increment the refcount).
        The callee's backward analysis observes Linear use of v
        with Cardinality = Once (no future duplication within
        the callee body); Access defaults to Owned at the
        parameter boundary per RL-32.
        Former DP-10 would conclude Uniqueness = Unique; this
        is unsound because the caller's upstream RcInc means
        multiple logical owner credits at the call site. Therefore DP-10 is rejected.

      Remaining derivation paths (proof that uniqueness is still
      derivable when genuinely true):
        Path A — fresh allocation per TF-3:
          Construct produces FRESH(shape) with Uniqueness = Unique
          at the definition site. No reliance on DP-10.
        Path B — IC-3 SCC fixpoint convergence:
          Parameter Uniqueness is established by the SCC fixpoint
          per IC-2 and IC-3; the join across all call sites is the
          uniqueness assigned to the parameter contract. When all
          call sites pass fresh-from-Construct values, the join
          remains Unique; otherwise it widens to MaybeShared. No
          reliance on DP-10.
        Path C — Reuse via TF-9 and CollectionReuse via TF-9a:
          Both produce FRESH outputs with Uniqueness = Unique;
          the reuse predicate (DP-6) requires Uniqueness not
          equal to Shared; this is independent of DP-10.

    Closed-world check:
      Every site in Annex E §AIMS (Forward Transfer) that
      writes Uniqueness writes either Unique (TF-3, TF-7, TF-9,
      TF-9a) or inherits from a contract (TF-5, TF-5a, TF-6,
      TF-6a, TF-6b, TF-6c) or inherits from source via Project
      (TF-4) or joins via Select (TF-8). None of these depend on
      DP-10. Therefore DP-10's removal does not remove any
      previously-derivable Uniqueness = Unique conclusion.

    Engines dispatched:
      refinement (post-condition implies pre-condition refinement
                  argument over the DP-10 removal)
      case_analysis (per remaining derivation path enumeration:
                     A, B, C exhausts the Uniqueness-write sites)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: 01A bootstrap discharged via §01A Implementation Items (engine constructive verification + Lean 4 cross-validation)
